          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is rigid polypropylene the default for parking?</h3>

          <p>
            It handles rolling loads well, it is light enough to lay quickly, and it comes up easily when you want it gone. The weak points are noise, since rigid tiles click as you walk across them, and a slight tendency to shift when the locking tabs are weak. Better brands answer both with thicker tabs and stiffer plastic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is flexible PVC the better choice?</h3>

          <p>
            When comfort and quiet matter more than price. PVC tiles feel more like a finished floor, and their weight helps them grip the slab. On the other hand, they cost more, some tires can stain them brown, and they expand and contract more with temperature than polypropylene. They make the most sense in gyms, workshops and finished garages.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you use rubber tiles where you park?</h3>

          <p>
            Generally not. Rubber is cushioned and excellent for standing or exercise, but under a car it can stain, soak up fluids and deform under point loads. Give rubber the gym corner and put a rigid or PVC tile in the parking area.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tiles-vs-coatings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Are Garage Floor Tiles Better Than Epoxy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tiles are better on damp, cracked or stained slabs and for anyone who will move or wants the garage back the same day; a coating is the better value on a sound, dry slab in a home you plan to keep. A coating bonds to the concrete, while a tile floor floats on top of it, and that single difference drives everything else. A coating needs a dry, sound, carefully prepared slab and days to cure. Tiles ask only for a reasonably flat, swept surface, and you can park on them the moment the last one clicks in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do tiles come out ahead?</h3>

          <p>
            On damaged or damp slabs. Cracks, stains, pits and patches vanish beneath them, and vented tiles let moisture evaporate instead of trapping it under a film. Tiles also win for renters and anyone moving soon, because the whole floor comes up and leaves with you. And they never get hot-tire pickup, the most common failure of a coated floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does a coating come out ahead?</h3>

          <p>
            On cost and cleaning. A DIY epoxy kit costs a fraction of what tiles cost per square foot, and one seamless surface is easier to mop than hundreds of seams. A coated floor is also quieter and leaves no gaps for dirt to settle into. To compare the coating options, see our guide to <a href="/best-garage-floor-coating/">garage floor coatings</a>, or our ranking of <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">epoxy kits</a> if a DIY coat appeals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is a simple way to decide?</h3>

          <p>
            Ask three questions. Does the slab pass a moisture test? Is it free of serious damage? Will you stay in the house? If all three answers are yes, a quality coating is the best value. If the slab is damp, cracked or stained past saving, or you want the floor back the same afternoon, tiles are the better buy despite costing more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ordering" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Garage Floor Tiles Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            With 12-inch tiles you need about one tile per square foot of floor plus 5 to 10 percent for cuts, so a 400 to 500 sq ft two-car garage takes roughly 420 to 550 tiles. Measure the floor at its widest and longest points, wall to wall and from the back wall to the outside edge of the garage door, then multiply. Larger tiles need fewer pieces: divide the area by the size of one tile in square feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much extra should you order?</h3>

          <p>
            Add about 5 percent for a simple rectangle and about 10 percent for a garage with steps, posts or an irregular outline. The extra covers cuts, mistakes and the odd tile you will want to replace years from now.
          </p>

