          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do you need edge pieces and ramps?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, on every exposed side. Most systems sell beveled edges for the garage door threshold and any open side, so tires roll on smoothly and nobody trips on the lip. Count the linear feet of exposed edge, which is at least the width of the garage door, and add a corner piece wherever two edges meet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you plan a border or checkerboard?</h3>

          <p>
            Lay the design out before you order. Sketch the floor on graph paper with one square per tile, then count each color separately. Order the whole floor from a single batch so the colors match.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you tile just part of the garage?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, and it is a good way to stretch a tight budget. Tile only the parking pads and seal the rest of the slab. A clear sealer from our list of <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealers</a> keeps dust down on the bare concrete while the tiles take care of the tire zones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Install Interlocking Garage Floor Tiles?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Empty and clean the slab, start at the most visible back corner, snap rows together toward the door, leave an expansion gap at the walls, and finish the door edge with ramps. Most owners lay a two-car garage in a day, and there is no cure time before you park.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How clean does the slab need to be?</h3>

          <p>
            Clean enough that nothing gritty or greasy is left. Empty the garage completely, sweep well and remove oil and grease. Tiles hide stains but do not stop them spreading or smelling, and grit trapped underneath makes tiles rock and click. A quick pass with a degreaser and a rinse, as our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">removing oil from concrete</a> explains, is time well spent. Let the slab dry fully before laying any adhesive-backed product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where should you start laying tiles?</h3>

          <p>
            Most installers begin in the back corner that is most on show and work toward the door, so the cut tiles end up at the door edge where the ramps cover them, or along a wall that cabinets will hide. Check which way the locking tabs face: most systems need the tiles laid in one direction so the loops drop over the hooks. Read the instructions before snapping down the first row.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much gap should you leave at the walls?</h3>

          <p>
            Roughly a quarter to a half inch at walls, steps and posts, or whatever the maker specifies, because plastic expands as it warms. Tiles fitted tight against the walls can buckle on a hot summer afternoon. Baseboards, cabinets or simply the shadow along the wall line hide the gap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you cut tiles and finish the edges?</h3>

          <p>
            Mark each cut with a straightedge and cut rigid tiles with a jigsaw or circular saw fitted with a fine-tooth blade. Flexible PVC cuts with a sharp utility knife scored several times. Finish the garage door edge with ramps, then check that the door closes onto the ramp without binding; if it does not, adjust the door's limit setting so the seal still meets the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Do Garage Floor Tiles Trap Moisture Underneath?
          </h2>

          <p>
            They can. Tiles do not create moisture, but a solid-top tile over a damp slab slows evaporation, and in a humid garage that can mean musty smells or mildew underneath. Vented tiles let air and water move, which helps the slab dry out, so if your slab shows moisture, pick a vented tile and keep the garage ventilated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do vented tiles cope with snow and slush?</h3>

          <p>
            Well. Melting slush drops through to the slab and drains away instead of forming puddles on top. The trade-off is that grit and salt residue build up beneath the tiles, so plan to lift a few rows each spring, hose the slab and let it dry before snapping them back down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about floor drains and slope?</h3>

          <p>
            Keep any floor drain reachable. Some brands sell drain tiles, or you can cut a removable section over it. Also confirm that the slab slopes toward the door or drain, so water does not pool under the tiles along the back wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you deal with persistent dampness?</h3>

          <p>
            Solve it at the source. Check the gutters and downspouts, the grading around the garage and the condition of the door seal. An attached garage that stays humid can benefit from a dehumidifier; see our pick of <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifiers</a> that also work well in garages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="loads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Can Garage Floor Tiles Handle Jack Stands and Heavy Loads?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A car's weight is no problem, but jack stands, kickstands and floor jack wheels need a pad under them. Most quality rigid tiles carry manufacturer load claims well beyond the weight of a passenger car or truck, and a vehicle's weight spread across four tires is easily handled. Point loads are another story: a jack stand, a motorcycle kickstand or the narrow wheel of a floor jack concentrates force on a small spot and can crack or dent a tile.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you protect tiles from point loads?</h3>

          <p>
            Put a plywood pad or a steel plate under jack stands and kickstands. Most installers also suggest lifting the car with the jack sitting on a pad rather than rolling it across the tiles under full load. If a tile does crack, the beauty of a modular floor is that you unsnap it and click in a new one, which is a good reason to keep a few spares from the original order.
          </p>

