          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why do some tiles get brown tire marks?</h3>

          <p>
            It is mostly a PVC issue. Some tire compounds react with vinyl over time and leave brown or yellow stains that will not wash off, a problem often called plasticizer migration. It shows up mainly under parked tires, and polypropylene tiles are much less prone to it. If you choose PVC, consider tire mats or a different color under the parking spots.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What makes tiles shift or separate?</h3>

          <p>
            Turning the steering wheel while the car is standing still. It twists the tiles under the front tires and is the most common reason tiles move or pull apart. Stronger locking systems resist it, and avoiding stationary steering helps any floor last longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Do Garage Floor Tiles Expand in Heat and Cold?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Garages are not climate controlled, and plastic tiles grow in summer and shrink and stiffen in winter. Leave the expansion gap the manufacturer recommends and, where you can, install on a moderate day so the floor is not laid at either extreme.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should tiles sit in the garage before installation?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, for about a day, so they reach the same temperature as the slab. Tiles carried in straight from a hot truck or a cold warehouse will change size after they are installed, which can open gaps or raise bumps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does deep cold affect tiles?</h3>

          <p>
            In very cold climates some rigid tiles become more brittle, so avoid dropping heavy tools on them in the depths of winter. Flexible PVC stiffens in the cold and gets harder to lock together, another reason to install in mild weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does tile color affect how hot it gets?</h3>

          <p>
            It does. Sun through an open door can heat dark tiles noticeably. Lighter colors stay cooler and show less dust, while darker ones hide stains better. Many owners settle on a mid-gray for the parking areas with a contrasting border for looks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Are Garage Floor Tiles Not Worth the Money?
          </h2>

          <p>
            When the slab is sound and dry, when the garage is little more than a walk-through, or when the floor is a pre-sale upgrade. Tiles are the most forgiving garage floor, but they are not always the smartest place to spend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if the slab is sound and you are staying?</h3>

          <p>
            On a sound, dry, newer slab in a house you plan to keep, a well-applied coating gives you a seamless floor for a fraction of the price. A two-car garage in premium vented tiles can cost as much as a professional coating job, and several times what a careful DIY epoxy install costs in materials.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if the garage is mostly storage?</h3>

          <p>
            Then tiles are hard to justify. If the space holds bikes, bins and a lawn mower and nobody stands or works there for long, a clear penetrating sealer or a one-part floor paint keeps the dust down and looks tidy for very little money. Spending three thousand dollars on tiles nobody notices makes little sense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about a workshop with heavy rolling equipment?</h3>

          <p>
            It can go either way. Tiles take dropped tools better than a thin coating, but the seams catch small casters, and a rolling engine hoist or a heavy welding cart puts real strain on the locking tabs. If that kind of equipment moves every day, a thick-build coating, or tiles under the parking bays only, often works better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do tiles help when you sell?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually less than a coating does. Buyers generally see a clean, coated or sealed floor as a feature, while tiles are personal and some buyers find the colors or patterns dated. If the floor is mainly for your own enjoyment, buy what you like. If it is a pre-sale upgrade, a neutral coating or a simple clean and seal usually returns more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pattern" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Which Tile Pattern and Color Is Best for a Garage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most garages, a vented or ribbed tile near the door and a mid-gray color overall work best, because they handle water and hide dirt. The top surface of a tile is more than decoration: it changes traction, cleaning and comfort, and the color decides how the floor looks after a year of use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coin, diamond, ribbed or smooth: what is the difference?</h3>

          <p>
            Coin tops, with rows of raised discs, are comfortable to stand on and grip well, but dirt settles between the discs and calls for a stiff broom. Diamond-plate tops look industrial, hide scuffs and wipe clean reasonably well. Ribbed and vented tops drain water and grip best in wet weather but are the hardest to roll small casters across. Smooth tops, found mostly on flexible PVC and peel-and-stick products, are the easiest to mop and the best for rolling chairs, carts and creepers, though they are also the slickest when wet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What layout works in a mixed-use garage?</h3>

          <p>
            A common compromise is a vented or ribbed tile in the parking bays near the door, where rain and snow land, and a smooth or coin tile in the dry workshop area at the back. Each zone gets the surface that suits what happens there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which colors hide dirt best?</h3>

          <p>
            Mid-grays hide dust, tire marks and salt residue best. Black hides oil but shows dust and road salt plainly. White and light colors brighten a dim garage and make dropped screws easy to spot, but they show every tire track. Bright reds, blues and yellows work best as borders or accents rather than whole floors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you avoid a color surprise?</h3>

          <p>
            Order a sample pack, or a few tiles in each color, and leave them on the floor for a week. Look at them in morning and evening light, park on them and spill a little water. It costs little and saves you from the expensive shock of a floor that looks different at full scale than it did on a website.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mixed-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Can You Use Garage Floor Tiles for a Home Gym or Workshop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and tiles make it easy to zone a garage that does double duty. The parking half can take rigid vented tiles, a gym corner can take thick rubber, and a workbench area can take a smooth PVC surface for rolling a stool. Because each zone is simply a different tile, you can rearrange the layout later without starting over.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do you need under free weights?</h3>

          <p>
            More than rigid tiles. On their own they are too hard for dropped weights: they can crack, and the impact passes straight to the slab. Lay dense rubber mats of at least three-eighths to three-quarters of an inch over the tiles, or swap that section for rubber tiles. Platform-style deadlift areas need still more protection, usually stacked rubber over plywood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which tiles suit a workshop?</h3>

          <p>
            Think about what rolls. Cabinet casters, creepers and mechanic stools move best on smooth or fine coin surfaces, while coarse ribbed tiles make them stutter. Welding is a special case: hot spatter can melt plastic, so keep the welding area on bare concrete or a fireproof mat and never weld over plastic tiles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do tiles help with standing fatigue?</h3>

          <p>
            The softer ones do. Hours on bare concrete are tiring, and flexible PVC or rubber tiles under the workbench make long sessions noticeably easier on the feet and back. A small spend there pays off more than upgrading the tiles under the car, which spends most of its life sitting still.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. How Do You Clean Garage Floor Tiles, and How Long Do They Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweep or blow them off regularly, mop solid tops, vacuum vented ones, and lift sections once or twice a year to clean underneath. Looked after that way, quality rigid tiles commonly last many years and can move with you to the next house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the everyday cleaning routine?</h3>

          <p>
            Sweep or use a leaf blower to clear dust and leaves. Mop solid-top tiles with warm water and a mild cleaner. On vented tiles, a wet-dry vacuum pulls debris out of the openings; our list of <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vacs</a> includes models that handle the job. Skip harsh solvents, which can dull or soften the plastic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you do about oil leaks?</h3>

          <p>
            Wipe oil and fluid spills promptly. On vented tiles they fall through to the slab, so set a drip tray or mat under any vehicle that leaks. Fluids left on the concrete under tiles can smell and spread if ignored.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How often should you lift the tiles?</h3>

          <p>
            Once or twice a year, and especially in winter climates. It takes an hour or two in a two-car garage and stops grit from wearing away the undersides of the tiles and the slab beneath.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long do garage floor tiles last?</h3>

          <p>
            Quality rigid tiles commonly last many years, often longer than coatings in heavy-use garages, and they can come with you when you move. Budget tiles may need replacing sooner where cars turn or park. Because single tiles can be swapped out, the floor wears gradually instead of failing all at once.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you store spare tiles?</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the spares from your original order stacked flat in a dry corner, out of the sun. Plastic fades slightly after years of light, so a spare kept in the dark may be a touch brighter than the floor. For the closest match, put the spare in a spot under a cabinet and move the faded tile from there into the damaged area.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes With Garage Floor Tiles
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Butting tiles tight against the walls</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plastic grows as it warms, so a floor fitted wall to wall on a cool day can buckle by midsummer. Leave the gap the maker specifies, usually about a quarter to half an inch, at walls, steps and posts. Baseboards or cabinets hide it, and the floor stays flat through every season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying tiles over a dirty slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tiles cover stains, but they rest on whatever is underneath. Grit makes them rock and click, oil can smell in warm weather, and debris wears the undersides. Sweep, degrease and let the slab dry before you start. It takes about an hour and leaves a quieter, steadier floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing solid tops in snow country</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solid-top tiles hold melting slush on the surface, where it spreads, gets tracked into the house and makes the floor slick. Where winters are snowy, put vented tiles in the parking bays, or add a containment mat under each car, so water drains toward the door instead of pooling on the tiles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering only the measured square footage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cuts at walls, steps and posts eat tiles, and a tile cracked years later needs a matching replacement. Order 5 to 10 percent extra from the same batch so the colors agree, and keep the leftovers, since a tile from a later order may be a slightly different shade. Spares cost little to store.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting jack stands straight onto the tiles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A jack stand focuses a vehicle's weight onto a footprint small enough to crack or dent a plastic tile. Set a plywood pad or steel plate under every stand and under motorcycle kickstands, and do the same for heavy equipment on narrow wheels. Hang the pads nearby so they actually get used.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I choose tiles or an epoxy coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Let the slab and your plans decide. Tiles suit damp, cracked or stained slabs, renters and anyone who wants the garage back the same day. Epoxy costs less, is seamless and mops more easily on a sound, dry slab. Tiles never suffer hot-tire pickup, and a coating never shifts or clicks underfoot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to tile a two-car garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For 400 to 500 square feet, plan on roughly $900 to $3,000 in materials at the time of writing, depending on brand and type, plus edges and ramps. Budget rigid tiles sit at the bottom of that range and premium vented tiles from brands such as Swisstrax at the top. Most owners lay the floor in a day, so labor costs little.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I lay tiles over cracked concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Minor cracks and stains are fine, and covering them is one of the main reasons people buy tiles. The slab does need to be reasonably flat, because large heaves or sunken sections make tiles rock. Fill deep holes and trip-height cracks with a patching compound so the tiles sit level, and sweep thoroughly before you start.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do garage tiles cause mold underneath?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tiles do not cause mold, but solid tiles on a damp slab in a humid garage can hold moisture in and lead to musty smells. Vented tiles allow airflow and drainage. Fix the moisture source, keep the garage ventilated, and lift sections now and then to clean and dry the slab. In humid areas, a dehumidifier helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will tiles move when I drive on them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Good tiles with stiff locking tabs stay put under normal driving. Budget tiles can creep where tires turn while the car is parked. Avoid turning the steering wheel when stationary, and if shifting starts, a little construction adhesive at the door threshold or a heavier tile in the turning zone helps. Better tiles simply shift less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are garage tiles slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Smooth and diamond-plate tops can be slick when wet. Coin and ribbed patterns grip better, and vented tiles drain water away from the surface. In snowy climates, use vented tiles near the door and in the parking bays, and keep smooth peel-and-stick PVC for dry areas. Entry mats help in winter, and coin tops are a good compromise in mixed-use garages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take garage tiles with me when I move?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if they interlock: they unsnap and pack into boxes. Clean them, stack them flat and label sections if you laid a pattern. Edges and ramps come up just as easily. Peel-and-stick tiles are much harder to reuse because the adhesive bonds to the slab, so renters and anyone planning to move should choose interlocking tiles.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

